% Generated by hand from roxygen2 comments in R/nutrigon_fit.R
\name{nutrigon_fit}
\alias{nutrigon_fit}
\title{Fit a quadratic response surface and locate the nutritional optimum}
\usage{
nutrigon_fit(data, x_col, y_col, z_col, design_id_col = NULL,
  model_type = c("lm", "glm", "lmer"), re_formula = NULL,
  re_group_cols = NULL, peak = TRUE, n_cloud = 500, n_reps = 5,
  region_quantile = NULL, ...)
}
\arguments{
\item{data}{A data frame containing nutrient intake and trait columns.}

\item{x_col}{Name of the protein (x-axis nutrient) column.}

\item{y_col}{Name of the carbohydrate (y-axis nutrient) column.}

\item{z_col}{Name of the trait (response) column.}

\item{design_id_col}{Name of a column identifying distinct design points
(e.g. treatment, or rail x concentration combination). Used for
within-design-point bootstrap resampling in \code{\link{nutrigon_compare}}.
If \code{NULL}, plain case-level resampling is used there instead.}

\item{model_type}{One of \code{"lm"} (default), \code{"glm"}, or
\code{"lmer"}. See Details.}

\item{re_formula}{For \code{model_type = "lmer"} only: the random-effects
portion of the model formula, as a string starting with \code{"+"}, e.g.
\code{"+ (1 | block)"}. Required when \code{model_type = "lmer"};
ignored otherwise. Any valid \pkg{lme4} random-effects syntax is
supported -- multiple/crossed terms
(\code{"+ (1 | block) + (1 | batch)"}), nesting
(\code{"+ (1 | block/subblock)"}), interaction grouping factors
(\code{"+ (1 | block:batch)"}), and random slopes -- since this string
is passed to \pkg{lme4} essentially unmodified. \strong{For random
slopes}, refer to the nutrient columns as \code{.x} and \code{.y} (not
your original \code{x_col}/\code{y_col} names), e.g.
\code{"+ (1 + .x | block)"}: internally the fitting data frame renames
your columns to \code{.x}/\code{.y}/\code{.z} before fitting, so
\code{"+ (1 + x | block)"} will fail with an \code{object 'x' not found}
error if your column was actually named \code{x_col = "protein"} or
similar (this specific case is detected and a translated error message
suggests the fix). Random \emph{intercepts} (\code{"+ (1 | block)"}) are
unaffected, since they don't reference the nutrient columns by name.}

\item{re_group_cols}{For \code{model_type = "lmer"} only: character
vector of column name(s) in \code{data} that \code{re_formula} refers to
as grouping factors (e.g. \code{"block"} for
\code{re_formula = "+ (1 | block)"}; both
\code{c("block", "batch")} for crossed terms or
\code{c("block", "subblock")} for nested terms). Required whenever
\code{re_formula} is supplied. Needed because internally the data is
reduced to just the x/y/z columns before fitting; any column
\code{re_formula} needs must be named here so it is carried through.}

\item{peak}{\code{TRUE} (default) to locate the maximum; \code{FALSE} for
the minimum.}

\item{n_cloud}{Number of candidate points for the Monte Carlo fallback.}

\item{n_reps}{Number of MC draws (the final region is the union of all
draws).}

\item{region_quantile}{Top (or bottom) quantile defining the peak region.
Default: 0.95 for peaks, 0.05 for valleys.}

\item{...}{Forwarded to the underlying fitter: e.g. \code{family = binomial()}
or \code{family = quasipoisson()} for \code{model_type = "glm"};
\code{weights = }, \code{control = }, etc. for \code{model_type = "glm"}
or \code{"lmer"}. Ignored for \code{model_type = "lm"}.}
}
\value{
An object of class \code{"nutrigon_fit"}, a list with components:
\describe{
  \item{optimum}{Data frame with \code{x}, \code{y}, \code{theta} (angle
    from origin, degrees), and \code{used_fallback} (logical).}
  \item{model}{A \code{nutrigon_*_fit} wrapper object. The raw fitted
    \code{lm}/\code{glm}/\code{merMod} object is available as
    \code{model$fit} (e.g. for \code{summary()}, residual diagnostics, or
    \code{AIC()}).}
  \item{model_type}{Character: \code{"lm"}, \code{"glm"}, or \code{"lmer"}.}
  \item{data}{The cleaned input data.}
  \item{hull}{Design hull vertices (for plotting).}
  \item{region}{The top/bottom quantile region (for plotting), on the
    link scale for \code{model_type = "glm"}.}
  \item{elongation}{Curvature elongation ratio (eigenvalue ratio of the
    Hessian), computed on the link scale.}
}
}
\description{
Fits a full quadratic response surface (\code{z ~ x + y + x^2 + y^2 + x:y})
to trait data collected under the Geometric Framework for Nutrition, and
locates the nutritional optimum (peak or valley) of the fitted surface.
The optimum is found in closed form when the fitted surface has the
correct curvature and the stationary point falls inside the convex hull
of the design; otherwise a Monte Carlo fallback samples points inside the
hull and takes the top (or bottom) quantile region.
}
\details{
Three model families are supported via \code{model_type}:
\describe{
  \item{\code{"lm"}}{(default) Ordinary least squares. Appropriate for
    approximately Gaussian, homoscedastic trait responses.}
  \item{\code{"glm"}}{Generalized linear model, any \code{family}
    (including quasi-families such as \code{quasipoisson()},
    \code{quasibinomial()} for overdispersed counts/proportions). Pass
    \code{family = } via \code{...}. The closed-form and Monte Carlo
    optimum-finding both operate on the \strong{link scale} (see below),
    which is the scale on which the surface is quadratic.}
  \item{\code{"lmer"}}{Linear mixed-effects model via \pkg{lme4} and
    \pkg{lmerTest} (both Suggests -- install with
    \code{install.packages(c("lme4", "lmerTest"))} if not already
    available). Requires \code{re_formula} specifying the random-effects
    structure, e.g. \code{re_formula = "+ (1 | block)"}. The optimum
    located is the population-level optimum (random effects integrated
    out via \code{re.form = NA}), not a group-specific optimum.}
}

For \code{model_type = "glm"}, the fitted linear predictor
\eqn{\eta(x,y) = \beta_0 + \beta_x x + \beta_y y + \beta_{xx} x^2 +
\beta_{yy} y^2 + \beta_{xy} xy} is quadratic in \eqn{(x,y)}, but the
predicted trait value on the response scale generally is not (e.g. for a
logit or log link). Because the link function is monotonic, the point
that maximises \eqn{\eta} also maximises the response-scale prediction,
so locating the optimum on the link scale and reporting it in the
original nutrient units loses nothing, and lets the same closed-form
stationary-point algebra be reused unchanged across \code{"lm"},
\code{"glm"}, and \code{"lmer"}.
}
\examples{
data(lee_drosophila)

# Default: ordinary least squares
fit_lifespan <- nutrigon_fit(lee_drosophila, "protein_eaten", "carb_eaten",
                              "lifespan", design_id_col = "treatment")
print(fit_lifespan)

\donttest{
# GLM example: a count-like trait fitted with a quasi-Poisson family
fit_eggs_glm <- nutrigon_fit(lee_drosophila, "protein_eaten", "carb_eaten",
                              "dailyeggs", design_id_col = "treatment",
                              model_type = "glm", family = quasipoisson())

# Mixed-model example (requires lme4 and lmerTest; install with
# install.packages(c("lme4", "lmerTest")) if not already available).
# re_group_cols must name the original column(s) re_formula refers to,
# since the fitter only sees the x/y/z columns by default.
fit_lifespan_lmer <- nutrigon_fit(
  lee_drosophila, "protein_eaten", "carb_eaten", "lifespan",
  model_type = "lmer", re_formula = "+ (1 | treatment)",
  re_group_cols = "treatment"
)
}

}
